# 3. Scale up

Diagram: https://github.com/luisfe05/holbertonschool-system_engineering-devops/blob/main/web_infrastructure_design/3-scale_up.png

## What happens when a user visits www.foobar.com

DNS resolves `www.foobar.com` to the load balancer cluster. The active HAProxy
receives the request and forwards it to the web server (Nginx). The web server
serves static files and passes dynamic requests to the application server,
which runs the code base and queries the database server. The response goes
back the same way to the user.

## Infrastructure

- 2 load balancers (HAProxy) configured as a cluster: one active and one
  passive (or both active), so the other takes over if one fails.
- 1 web server (Nginx) on its own server.
- 1 application server (with the code base) on its own server.
- 1 database server (MySQL) on its own server.

Compared with the previous design, this adds 1 server and 1 load balancer, and
the components are split so each one has its own server.

## Specifics

- **Why each additional element is added:**
  - *Second load balancer (HAProxy cluster):* removes the load balancer as a
    SPOF. Both HAProxy nodes share a virtual IP and monitor each other
    (heartbeat); if the active one fails, the other takes over.
  - *Additional server:* gives room to split the stack so each component can
    be given its own server.
  - *Split components (web server, application server, database each on its
    own server):* every component gets its own resources (CPU, RAM, disk), can
    be scaled, tuned and maintained independently, and a problem in one
    component does not take down the others.
- **Application server vs web server:** the web server (Nginx) handles HTTP
  and serves static content, while the application server runs the application
  code and produces dynamic content.
